e three  different confounding variables which  are causing D the the causal state and  there's the outcome which is also  dependent on these confounding variables  so we can just drop all this into a data  frame X here at the bottom if you look  at the naive effect just the difference  between the observe a tional treatment  and observational control groups what  you find is a point three six effect if  you compare it with the true effect just  by differencing the potential outcomes  which we know because we we generated  the data ourselves you find the true  effect is actually zero cool so if you  just drop this data into the propensity  score matching you tell it which is the  causal state D which is the outcome Y  and which are the covariance Z and  whether they're continuous or discrete  so it knows how to how to handle them  it'll give you the correct average  treatment effect so it just makes it  pretty quick and easy so it sort of just  works trademark if if you're smart  though you won't stop there because  there are assumptions that the the  algorithm has to satisfy rather the the  system has to satisfy those assumptions  in the context of propensity score  matching and this is actually true in a  lot of different contexts is that the  the Z variables have to have the same  domain for each causal state so when the  D equals to zero state the Z variables  have to have the same domain and  likewise for the the D equals to 1 state  cool so you can actually check that  there are a couple of methods for doing  that just to put that assumption  explicitly it's that this probability  has to be positive for all values of the  inputs  and you can just kind of drop some plots  out to check that visually at least in  1d so there's this check support method  that just generates these plots for you  you can also see how well you did at  balancing on the Z's which is another  kind of technical point of propensity  score matching so there's the kind of is  it worth it question because the biases  are so big you really need to do this if  you're working with observational data  but the time investment makes it  difficult so my argument is if we  develop the right tool set that does  automated assumption checking then it  should be low enough investment and  require low enough expertise that it  should be practical in in a realistic  context just to show one more example  here's an addition to the pandas  dataframe object called Z plot so  instead of just plotting you say Z plot  it takes exactly the same methods and  it'll give you a causal plot instead of  a a correlative plot so if you look at  the causal plot here for the same data  it reverses sine versus the regular old  plot method so this is called Simpsons  paradox where you get this sign reversal  between the different policies so you  can do this as easily as your normal  exploratory data analysis workflow so  this should you should kind of fit  seamlessly into the the usual data  scientist or analyst toolkit cool so  just kind of the last slide where we're  hiring for my team at Barclays so if  you'd like to join join me at work and  learn some of this stuff in in the real  world then just let me know if my email  is right there you could also search for  the job posting online cool I think  that's the that's it so thanks  you  [Music]


